<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1331">1331</a>–<a href="/years/1371">1371</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Alexander was tsar of the Second Bulgarian Empire from 1331 to 1371. He made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, recovered lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and presided over a cultural flowering at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, including the illuminated Tetraevangelion of 1355–1356 and the Bulgarian translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasses-chronicle">Manasses Chronicle</a>. By dividing his realm between his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a> at Tarnovo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, he left it weakened in the face of the Ottoman advance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria" width="302" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asenovec1186 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_ALexander.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Alexander was the son of Despot Sratsimir of Kran and of Petrica, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-shishman">Michael Asen III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and so a nephew of Michael Asen III and a descendant of the Asen dynasty. The date of his birth is unknown. By 1330 he was a despot governing the city of Lovech. That year he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-velbazhd">Battle of Velbazhd</a> alongside his father and his father-in-law Basarab of Wallachia, and Bulgaria was defeated. The defeat and worsening relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> brought an internal crisis, and in 1331 a coup drove Ivan Stefan out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> and the conspirators placed Ivan Alexander on the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1331 he reconquered north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> eased under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Uroš IV Dušan</a>, who allied with him and married his sister Helena of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1332. In the summer of 1332 the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III Palaiologos</a> advanced while a rebellion by Belaur broke out at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>. Ivan Alexander defeated the Byzantines at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rusokastro">Battle of Rusokastro</a> and made peace on the status quo. In 1344 the regency of John V Palaiologos ceded him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> and nine fortresses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodope-mountains">Rhodope Mountains</a> for his support in the Byzantine civil war, his last major foreign success.</p><p>Afterwards the empire suffered Turkish raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> and the loss of his sons Ivan Asen IV in 1349 and Michael Asen IV by 1355. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> in 1365 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a> captive, but Ivan Alexander, with money paid by John V after Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-vi-count-of-savoy">Amadeus VI of Savoy</a> had seized Bulgarian coastal cities, induced Dobrotica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> and Vladislav I of Wallachia to reconquer it in 1369. He also crowned sons of his second marriage, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a> and Ivan Asen V, as co-emperors, while Ivan Sratsimir became effectively independent around 1356.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ivan Alexander died on 17 February 1371 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>. His reign saw a cultural flowering, sometimes called the Second Golden Age of Bulgarian culture. He ordered many monasteries and churches built or renovated, and portraits of him as a donor survive at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bachkovo-monastery">Bachkovo Monastery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn Churches</a> of Ivanovo.</p><p>Works of the period include the Middle Bulgarian translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasses-chronicle">Manasses Chronicle</a> of 1344 to 1345, the Tetraevangelia of Ivan Alexander of 1355 to 1356, now in the British Library, and the Tomić Psalter of 1360. He convened councils in 1350 and 1359 to 1360 that condemned heresies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a>, and the writer Ivan Vazov made him the hero of a novelette and a drama.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1330 the Serbs defeated and killed Tsar Michael Shishman at the Battle of Velbazhd, though <b>Ivan Alexander</b> beat the Byzantines at Rusokastro in 1332.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tsar <b>Ivan Alexander</b> of the Second Bulgarian Empire made his younger son Ivan Shishman heir and gave his eldest, Ivan Sratsimir, the north-western region around Vidin.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsardom-of-vidin">Tsardom of Vidin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1351" aria-label="Veliko Tarnovo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 634 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5618 904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5618 904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1351">What was here in 1351? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
